<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        <a href="tax/review">Review tax suggestions</a> shows the suggested claims one at a time, large and clear, so you can go
        through a year's worth quickly, most of it from the keyboard. Open it from the <a href="tax">Tax report</a>.
    </p>
    <HelpClip Name="tax-review" Caption="Claiming suggestions with C and dismissing one with N." />

    <h3>Deciding each one</h3>
    <p>
        Each card shows the date, account, amount, description and category, and the tax category suggested with a note on
        what it covers. Then:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Claim it</span> (<kbd>C</kbd>): it supports a claim; it moves into the confirmed total.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Not claimable</span> (<kbd>N</kbd>): it isn't; it's taken off the report and not suggested again.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Skip</span> (<kbd>S</kbd> or <kbd>→</kbd>): decide later. <span class="help-ui">Back</span> (<kbd>←</kbd>) returns to the previous one.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Claim under a different tax category</span>: right transaction, wrong category, such as a pharmacy
            purchase that's really a disability support.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The bar at the top shows how many are left, claimed and not claimable.</p>

    <h3>Might have been missed</h3>
    <p>
        Below the cards is spending from that year with no tax treatment, in the categories where claims tend to hide: Health
        &amp; Fitness, Gifts &amp; Donations, Education, and Kids &amp; Family. Most of it won't be claimable, but prescriptions,
        therapy, donations, tuition and child care sometimes slip through. Choose a tax category in the
        <span class="help-ui">Tax</span> column to claim one, or <em>Not claimable</em>.
    </p>
    <p class="help-note">
        Suggestions are deliberately broad. A suggestion isn't a sign that something is claimable; your receipts and the CRA's
        rules decide that.
    </p>
</div>
